  <setup>
    <selectFromSequence name="a" from="12" to="18" step="1"/>
    <selectFromSequence name="b" from="0.25" to="0.75" step="0.25"/>
    <function name="h" symbolic="true" domain="[0,$lands]">
      -4.9x^2 + $a
    </function>
    <boolean name="Apeak">$A.x = 0</boolean>
    <boolean name="Bpeak">$B.x = 0</boolean>
    <math name="lands" simplify>sqrt($a/4.9)</math>




    <!-- neither of these points can start at x=0 while I'm hiding the label at the peak, or else the label's latex will not be rendered when the label becomes visible.  But if it starts visible, the latex renders fine.  It would be better to have some masking to make the label prominent in front of the axes instead of the hack I'm using with conditional content to move the label if it would appear on the axes.  -->
    <point name="A" styleNumber="2" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">
      (1.05,$$h(.1.05))
      <constrainTo>$h</constrainTo>
      <attractToGrid dy="0.01" dx="0.125" xthreshold=".01" />
      <label hide="$Apeak"><m>$A.coords</m></label>
    </point>

    <point name="B" styleNumber="2" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">
      (1.38,$$h(1.38))
      <constrainTo>$h</constrainTo>
      <attractToGrid dy="0.01" dx="0.125" xthreshold=".01" />
      <label hide="$Bpeak"><m>$B.coords</m></label>
    </point>

  </setup>
  <p>
    A ball is dropped from a window.  The ball's height (in meters) at time <m>t</m> (in seconds) is given by  the function <m>h(t)</m>, whose graph is shown below.
  </p>

    <graph xmin="-1" xmax="3" ymin="-2" ymax="20" size="medium" grid="false" showNavigation="false" fixAxes="true">
      <shortDescription>a quadratic function that opens down with vertex at t=0 that represents the height of a falling ball</shortDescription>
      <description>Plot of <m>y = h(t)</m>, which measures the height in meters of the falling ball at time <m>t</m> in seconds.</description>
      $h $A $B

      <!-- the code below makes the labels visible and in approximately the right position no matter whether t=0 or not -->
      <conditionalContent condition="$Apeak">
        <label anchor="(-0.3,$a+.4)"><m>$A.coords</m></label>
      </conditionalContent>
      <!--<conditionalContent condition="!$Apeak">
        <label anchor="($A.x+.1,$A.y+.2)" positionFromAnchor="upperRight"><m>$A.coords</m></label>
      </conditionalContent>-->
      <conditionalContent condition="$Bpeak">
        <label anchor="(-0.3,$a+.4)"><m>$B.coords</m></label>
      </conditionalContent>
      <!--<conditionalContent condition="$!Bpeak">
        <label anchor="($B.x+.1,$B.y+.2)" positionFromAnchor="upperRight"><m>$B.coords</m></label>
      </conditionalContent>   -->
    </graph>

  <ol marker="a">
    <li>
      <p> What are the units on the average velocity of the ball? </p>
        <answer>
          <choiceInput name="choice1" inline>
            <shortDescription>units of average velocity</shortDescription>
            <choice>meters per hour</choice>
            <choice>meters</choice>
            <choice>feet per second</choice>
            <choice credit="1">meters per second</choice>
            <choice>feet</choice>
          </choiceInput>
        </answer>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Note that by dragging points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> on the graph, you can see the approximate coordinates of each point.  Find the average velocity of the ball on the interval <m><math simplify>$b</math> \le t \le <math simplify>2*$b</math>.</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><shortDescription>average velocity over an interval</shortDescription>
            ($$h(2*$b)-$$h($b))/($b)
        </answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Find the average velocity of the ball on the interval from the time the ball is dropped to the time the ball lands. </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.05" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
          <shortDescription>average velocity over an interval</shortDescription>
            (0-$$h(0))/(sqrt($a/4.9))
        </answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>